```

After authentication, the user is seamlessly redirected to the attacker-controlled domain. This is a classic phishing vector — the user trusts the initial domain and has just authenticated, making them highly susceptible to credential harvesting or malware delivery on the target site.

The default fallback `"/dashboard"` on line 2 only applies when the parameter is absent or empty; it does not protect against malicious values.

**Remediation:**

Validate that `returnTo` is a relative path (same-origin) before redirecting:

```typescript
app.get("/login/callback", (req, res) => {
  const returnTo = req.query.returnTo as string || "/dashboard";

  // Allow only relative paths — block protocol-relative and absolute URLs
  const isRelative = returnTo.startsWith("/") && !returnTo.startsWith("//");
  const safeDest = isRelative ? returnTo : "/dashboard";

  // Authenticate user...
  res.redirect(safeDest);
});
```

Alternatively, maintain an allowlist of permitted redirect destinations.

