### PERF-003: Recursive N+1 Query Pattern Without Depth Limit
**Severity:** High
**Location:** Lines 23–32

```javascript
async function getChildren(parentId) {
  const { rows } = await pool.query(
    'SELECT * FROM categories WHERE parent_id = $1', [parentId]
  );
  for (const row of rows) {
    row.children = await getChildren(row.id);
  }
  return rows;
}
```

This issues **one SQL query per node** in the category tree. A tree with 500 nodes produces 500 sequential round-trips to PostgreSQL. Each round-trip has network + parse + plan overhead (~0.5–2 ms minimum), so even a modest tree causes 250 ms–1 s of pure latency from query overhead alone.

Additionally, there is no depth limit — a corrupted row with a circular parent reference (`A → B → A`) will cause infinite recursion, eventually crashing the process with a stack overflow.

**Recommendation:** Use a single recursive CTE:

```javascript
app.get('/api/categories/:id/tree', async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await pool.query(`
    WITH RECURSIVE tree AS (
      SELECT id, name, parent_id, 0 AS depth
      FROM categories WHERE parent_id = $1
      UNION ALL
      SELECT c.id, c.name, c.parent_id, t.depth + 1
      FROM categories c
      JOIN tree t ON c.parent_id = t.id
      WHERE t.depth < 10  -- depth limit prevents runaway recursion
    )
    SELECT * FROM tree ORDER BY depth, name
  `, [req.params.id]);

  // Assemble tree in-memory from flat result (single pass)
  const map = new Map();
  const roots = [];
  for (const row of rows) {
    row.children = [];
    map.set(row.id, row);
    if (row.parent_id === parseInt(req.params.id)) {
      roots.push(row);
    } else {
      map.get(row.parent_id)?.children.push(row);
    }
  }
  res.json(roots);
});
```

This replaces N queries with 1 query and bounds recursion depth at the database level.

