**Description:**

The code comment on line 4 itself acknowledges the problem: *"When cache expires, ALL concurrent requests hit the database."* This is a textbook cache stampede (thundering herd) vulnerability. When the 60-second TTL expires for a popular product, every concurrent request that arrives before the cache is repopulated will independently execute `db.query("SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = $1", [id])` on line 5. For a high-traffic product endpoint, this can cause a sudden spike of identical queries hitting the database simultaneously.

**Evidence:**

- **Line 2–3:** Cache lookup — all concurrent requests will get a miss at roughly the same time after TTL expiry.
- **Line 5:** Each request independently queries the DB with the same `id`.
- **Line 6:** Each request independently writes back to Redis, duplicating work.
- There is no mutex, lock, singleflight/coalescing, or probabilistic early expiration (PER) mechanism present.

**Recommendation:**

Implement a locking/coalescing pattern so that only one request recomputes the value while others wait:

```typescript
async function getProduct(id: string): Promise<Product> {
  const cacheKey = `product:${id}`;
  const lockKey = `lock:product:${id}`;

  const cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  // Attempt to acquire a short-lived lock
  const acquired = await redis.set(lockKey, "1", "EX", 5, "NX");

  if (acquired) {
    try {
      const product = await db.query("SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = $1", [id]);
      await redis.set(cacheKey, JSON.stringify(product), "EX", 60);
      return product;
    } finally {
      await redis.del(lockKey);
    }
  } else {
    // Wait briefly, then retry from cache
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 100));
    return getProduct(id);
  }
}
```

Alternatively, consider probabilistic early expiration (XFetch algorithm) to stagger recomputation before TTL actually expires.

