# Software Practices Evaluation

## Findings

### SWDEV-001: Excessive Nesting Depth (Arrow Anti-Pattern)

**Severity:** High
**Category:** Code Quality & Readability
**Location:** Lines 2–28, nesting reaches **14 levels deep**

**Evidence:** The function consists of a single chain of nested `if/else` blocks, each adding one indentation level. The actual business logic (`createUser`) is buried at indentation level 14 (line 15). Every validation check adds a new nesting level rather than using early returns — this is the textbook "arrow anti-pattern" (also called "pyramid of doom").

**Why this is a problem:**
- **Readability:** A developer must mentally track 14 levels of conditional context to understand when `createUser` is reached. The matching `else` clauses at lines 16–27 are far removed from their corresponding `if`, making the error responses nearly impossible to map back to their conditions without an IDE.
- **Maintainability:** Adding a new validation (e.g., checking `age`) requires pushing the entire block one level deeper and adding another `else` at the bottom. This violates the **Open/Closed Principle** — extending the validation logic requires modifying the structure of the entire function.
- **Bug risk:** The inverted ordering of else clauses (bottom-up) relative to the if clauses (top-down) is a concrete source of copy-paste errors when this code is modified.

**Recommendation:** Replace nested conditions with **guard clauses** (early returns). Each validation becomes a flat, sequential check. This is the standard refactoring prescribed by Robert C. Martin (*Clean Code*, Chapter 5) and Martin Fowler (*Refactoring*, "Replace Nested Conditional with Guard Clauses").

```typescript
export function processRequest(req: Request): Response {
  if (req.method !== "POST") {
    return error("Wrong method");
  }
  if (req.headers.contentType !== "application/json") {
    return error("Wrong content type");
  }
  if (!req.body) {
    return error("Missing body");
  }
  if (!req.body.action) {
    return error("Missing action");
  }
  if (req.body.action !== "create") {
    return error("Unknown action");
  }
  if (!req.body.data) {
    return error("Missing data");
  }
  if (!req.body.data.name) {
    return error("Missing name");
  }
  if (req.body.data.name.length === 0) {
    return error("Name empty");
  }
  if (req.body.data.name.length >= 256) {
    return error("Name too long");
  }
  if (!req.body.data.email) {
    return error("Missing email");
  }
  if (!isValidEmail(req.body.data.email)) {
    return error("Invalid email");
  }
  if (!req.body.data.password) {
    return error("Missing password");
  }
  if (req.body.data.password.length < 8) {
    return error("Password too short");
  }

  return createUser(req.body.data);
}
```

This refactored version:
- Is **flat** (max 1 level of nesting) and reads top-to-bottom.
- Each guard clause is independently understandable and independently testable.
- Adding a new validation requires inserting a single `if/return` block — no structural changes to surrounding code.
- The happy path (`createUser`) is clearly visible at the bottom.

