### CYBER-001: Remote Code Execution via `eval()` on Unsanitized User Input

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **CWE** | [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 – Injection |
| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Location:** Line 4

```javascript
const result = eval(req.body.expression);
```

**Description:**

`eval()` is called directly on `req.body.expression`, which is entirely user-controlled input from the POST request body. This allows an attacker to execute **arbitrary JavaScript code on the server** with the full privileges of the Node.js process.

**Exploit scenario:**

An attacker sends:

```bash
curl -X POST http://target/calculate \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"expression": "require(\"child_process\").execSync(\"cat /etc/passwd\").toString()"}'
```

This would execute OS commands, enabling:
- **Full remote code execution (RCE)** — read/write files, install backdoors, pivot to internal networks
- **Data exfiltration** — access environment variables (`process.env`), database credentials, secrets
- **Denial of service** — `process.exit(1)` or infinite loops
- **Reverse shell** — establish persistent attacker access

This is not a theoretical risk; it is trivially exploitable with a single HTTP request.

**Remediation:**

Replace `eval()` with a safe mathematical expression parser. For example, using the [`mathjs`](https://mathjs.org/) library:

```javascript
const express = require("express");
const { evaluate } = require("mathjs");
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.post("/calculate", (req, res) => {
  const { expression } = req.body;

  if (typeof expression !== "string" || expression.length > 200) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Invalid expression" });
  }

  try {
    // mathjs.evaluate() safely parses math expressions
    // without executing arbitrary JS
    const result = evaluate(expression);
    res.json({ result });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: "Could not evaluate expression" });
  }
});
```

Alternatively, if only simple arithmetic is needed, use a whitelist approach:

```javascript
app.post("/calculate", (req, res) => {
  const { expression } = req.body;

  // Allow only digits, operators, parentheses, decimal points, whitespace
  if (!/^[\d+\-*/().\s]+$/.test(expression)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Invalid characters in expression" });
  }

  try {
    // Function constructor is still safer than eval in this
    // constrained context, but a dedicated parser is preferred
    const result = new Function(`"use strict"; return (${expression})`)();
    res.json({ result });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: "Could not evaluate expression" });
  }
});
```

> **Note:** The `Function` constructor approach with a strict regex whitelist is safer but still carries residual risk. A dedicated math parser library (`mathjs`, `expr-eval`) is the strongly preferred solution.

