| **Confidence** | 99% |

**Location:** Line 6

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

**Description:**
Both `username` and `password` are directly concatenated into the LDAP search filter without any sanitization or escaping. An attacker can inject arbitrary LDAP filter expressions to bypass authentication entirely.

**Exploit Example:**

An attacker supplies:
- `username`: `*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*`
- `password`: anything

This produces the filter:
```
(&(uid=*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

The first complete filter `(&(uid=*)(uid=*))` matches **every user** in the directory. Since `results.hasMore()` on line 10 only checks if at least one result exists, this returns `true` — granting authentication without valid credentials.

An even simpler attack: `username = *` and `password = *` produces `(&(uid=*)(userPassword=*))`, which matches all entries with any uid and any password.

**Remediation:**

Escape all LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`) in user-supplied values before filter construction. Use a proper escaping utility or, preferably, framework-provided parameterized search:

```java
import javax.naming.directory.SearchControls;
import javax.naming.ldap.LdapName;

public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        // Escape LDAP special characters
        String safeUsername = escapeLdapFilter(username);
        String safePassword = escapeLdapFilter(password);

        String filter = "(&(uid={0})(userPassword={1}))";
        Object[] filterArgs = new Object[]{safeUsername, safePassword};
        SearchControls sc = new SearchControls();
        sc.setSearchScope(SearchControls.SUBTREE_SCOPE);
        // Use the parameterized search overload
        NamingEnumeration<?> results = ctx.search("dc=example,dc=com", filter, filterArgs, sc);
        return results.hasMore();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return false;
    }
}

private static String escapeLdapFilter(String input) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '\\': sb.append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

The best approach is the `ctx.search(name, filterExpr, filterArgs, cons)` overload (shown above), which handles parameterization at the API level — analogous to parameterized SQL queries.

