### CYBER-001: Unsafe Deserialization of Untrusted Network Data (CWE-502)

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | 🔴 **CRITICAL** |
| **Location** | Lines 17-18 |
| **OWASP** | A08:2021 – Software and Data Integrity Failures |
| **CWE** | CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data |

**Description:**

Lines 17-18 directly deserialize arbitrary objects from a raw TCP socket with zero filtering:

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

Any remote client connecting to port 9999 can send a crafted serialized byte stream. Java's `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` will instantiate arbitrary classes available on the classpath before any application-level type check occurs. The `instanceof Command` check on line 30 happens **after** deserialization — the damage is already done.

**Exploit scenario:**

An attacker uses a tool like [ysoserial](https://github.com/frohoff/ysoserial) to generate a gadget chain payload (e.g., `CommonsCollections`, `Spring`, `JBossInterceptors`) targeting libraries on the server's classpath. Sending this payload to port 9999 achieves **Remote Code Execution (RCE)** — full server compromise with the privileges of the Java process.

This is not theoretical; Java deserialization RCE has been exploited at scale (CVE-2015-7501, CVE-2017-12149, CVE-2019-17571, among many others).

**Remediation:**

Replace raw `ObjectInputStream` with a filtering approach. At minimum, use `ObjectInputFilter` (Java 9+) to allowlist only expected classes:

```java
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
ois.setObjectInputFilter(filterInfo -> {
    if (filterInfo.serialClass() == null) {
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.UNDECIDED;
    }
    // Only allow the specific Command implementations you expect
    if (Command.class.isAssignableFrom(filterInfo.serialClass())) {
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
    }
    return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
});
Object obj = ois.readObject();
```

**Preferred approach:** Avoid Java native serialization entirely for network-facing services. Use a safe data format (JSON with Jackson/Gson, Protocol Buffers, etc.) and explicitly construct typed objects from validated fields.

