An attacker can craft a class implementing `Command` with any arbitrary logic in `execute()` — file deletion, reverse shell, data exfiltration — serialize an instance, and send it to port 9999. Even if CYBER-001's deserialization filter is partly addressed, this design explicitly invites the server to **run attacker-supplied code**.

**Remediation:**

Do not execute methods on deserialized objects representing untrusted logic. Instead, deserialize a **data-only** message (e.g., a command name string and parameters), then dispatch to a hardcoded set of server-side handlers:

```java
// Safe pattern: data-only message, server-side dispatch
record CommandMessage(String action, Map<String, String> params) {}

// Server-side handler map
Map<String, Consumer<Map<String, String>>> handlers = Map.of(
    "ping", params -> System.out.println("pong"),
    "status", params -> System.out.println("OK")
);

// Dispatch
var handler = handlers.get(message.action());
if (handler != null) handler.accept(message.params());
```

