### CYBER-001: LDAP Injection via Unsanitized User Input

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **CWE** | CWE-90 (LDAP Injection) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 — Injection |
| **Line** | 21 |

**Description:**

On line 21, both `username` and `password` are concatenated directly into an LDAP search filter string without any sanitization or escaping:

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

An attacker can inject arbitrary LDAP filter syntax to bypass authentication entirely.

**Exploit Scenario:**

An attacker supplies:
- `username`: `*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*`
- `password`: anything

This produces the filter:
```
(&(uid=*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

The first filter `(&(uid=*)(uid=*))` matches **every user** in the directory. Since `results.hasMore()` on line 26 only checks if at least one result exists, the attacker authenticates successfully without knowing any valid credentials.

Even simpler: `username = *` produces `(&(uid=*)(userPassword=...))` which, combined with a password wildcard `*`, yields `(&(uid=*)(userPassword=*))` — matching all entries with any password.

**Remediation:**

Escape all LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`, `/`) in user input before incorporating it into filters. Use a dedicated escaping method or, preferably, parameterized search via `javax.naming.directory.DirContext.search(String, String, Object[], SearchControls)`:

```java
// Use parameterized search — JNDI substitutes {0}, {1} safely
String filter = "(&(uid={0})(userPassword={1}))";
Object[] filterArgs = new Object[]{ username, password };
NamingEnumeration<?> results = ctx.search(BASE_DN, filter, filterArgs, sc);
```

Alternatively, manually escape per RFC 4515 §3:

```java
private static String escapeLdapFilter(String input) {
    if (input == null) return null;
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '\\': sb.append("\\5c"); break;
            case '*':  sb.append("\\2a"); break;
            case '(':  sb.append("\\28"); break;
            case ')':  sb.append("\\29"); break;
            case '\0': sb.append("\\00"); break;
            default:   sb.append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

