### CONC-002: No Rollback in `processOrder` — Partial Reservation on Failure

**Severity:** High
**Location:** Lines 17–25 (`processOrder` method)

**Evidence:**

`processOrder` iterates over items and reserves them sequentially. If reservation fails at item N (line 22), all previously reserved items (1 through N−1) remain reserved and are never released:

```typescript
for (const item of items) {
  const reserved = await this.reserveItem(item.id, item.qty);  // Line 20
  if (!reserved) {
    // Previous items already reserved but not rolled back          // Line 22
    throw new Error(`Insufficient stock for ${item.id}`);          // Line 23
  }
}
```

This is an atomicity/transactional integrity issue in a concurrent context. Under concurrent load, leaked reservations from partial orders permanently reduce available stock for other requests, compounding over time.

**Recommendation:**

Track successful reservations and implement compensating rollback on failure:

```typescript
async processOrder(items: Array<{ id: string; qty: number }>): Promise<void> {
  const reserved: Array<{ id: string; qty: number }> = [];
  try {
    for (const item of items) {
      const success = await this.reserveItem(item.id, item.qty);
      if (!success) {
        throw new Error(`Insufficient stock for ${item.id}`);
      }
      reserved.push(item);
    }
  } catch (err) {
    // Compensating rollback
    for (const item of reserved) {
      await this.releaseItem(item.id, item.qty);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```

